  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1257 PM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nWe're looking at another potential fog event tonight. Light winds \nand surface conditions near or at saturation could lead to dense fog \ndevelopment overnight tonight and tomorrow morning. High res models \nhave fog developing over Kansas and the eastern plains and a surface \ncyclone to the northeast of Denver. This circulation could usher\nfog right into the urban corridor from the north. High res \nensembles currently have a medium to high chance (50% to 80%) that\npatchy dense fog (vis <0.5mi) will develop across the urban \ncorridor, northern I-25 corridor, and portions of the eastern \nplains. This could lead to dangerous travel conditions for the \nFriday morning commute. A similar fog setup will be in place for \nSaturday morning, leading to additional fog and travel concerns \nfor portions of the urban corridor and eastern plains for Saturday\nmorning. \n\nA shortwave will move to the southeast of the area on Friday. While \nmost of the forcing and precipitation from the shortwave will be to \nour east/southeast, our far eastern counties could be clipped by a \nweak shower or two. No impacts are expected. On Saturday, another \nshortwave will move through the southwest flow aloft bringing \nisolated to scattered showers to our higher terrain in the \nafternoon.\n\nOn Sunday, things start to get more uncertain. Late Saturday into \nearly Sunday there will be two upper level low pressure systems off \nthe west coast of North America. One will be off the coast of Baja \nCalifornia while the other will be closer to the Pacific Northwest. \nThe southern one will bring significant moisture transport into the \nfour corners region on Sunday, bringing our PW values into the 200% \nto 300% of normal range for Sunday and Monday. The northern system \nwill dig southeast, phasing with the other system sometime Sunday or \nearly Monday. Models tend to struggle with exactly how two troughs \nphase together, which brings high uncertainty on the exact path of\nthe resulting system and the potential impacts for our area. \n\nThere will be widespread upper level support for showers ahead of \nthe approaching trough. Right now, peak QG forcing looks to be \nMonday afternoon, which is when we'd expect the most widespread \nshowers. However the timing and strength of the trough could very \nwell change between now and late this weekend. A strong surface \nfront will move through sometime late Sunday and Monday, bringing \nstrong north/northwesterly winds to the plains. The current forecast \nupper level and lower level winds, don't look great for \nadditional topographic forcing and higher precip totals, with the \nexception of maybe the Palmer, which tends to do better with \nnortherly winds than the rest of our area. However, we will be \nkeeping an eye on the development of the surface low and any \npotential eastward turning of the winds, which would impact precip\ntotals in our area. Currently, liquid precip amounts look to stay\nunder an inch for our area, with much of the area remaining below\nhalf an inch. In terms of snow, snow levels are forecast to start\nout around 10kft or 11kft, dropping closer to 7,500ft by Tuesday \nmorning. Models remain too warm for snow on the plains, with the \nNBM giving the plains a 0% chance of any measurable snowfall. \n\nThis system will move off to the east on Tuesday, bringing weak upper \nlevel ridging and drier conditions. Northerly surface winds will \ncontinue for Tuesday and Wednesday, helping keep temperatures near or \nbelow normal.",
